From c0450a4c8aaa84fbabe2e5f499cd00f16b51e8f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yihou <yihou@amd.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 14:45:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 21/25] test(router): behavioural coverage for the bigram
 kv-event decode

PR #56 covers the shape end-to-end through the publish path. These go at the
seam instead, because the failure mode is silent and the earlier revert check
showed why that distinction matters: importing _flat_tokens by name makes a
revert produce an ImportError, every test in the module errors out, and the
suite reports "fails without the fix" while proving only that a symbol is
absent -- which is trivial and says nothing about behaviour.

So the helper is fetched via getattr and only its own unit tests skip when it is
missing. The three behavioural tests still execute on pre-fix code and fail for
three distinct reasons: the hashes are wrong, the bigram and plain views
disagree where they must agree, and the wire decode rejects pairs outright.

The second is the one that carries weight. It is the same assertion the
experiment made against the live router: a bigram view must hash IDENTICALLY to
the plain-int view of the same tokens, not merely be non-empty. Measured 51
blocks on the prefill worker under MTP, byte-identical to the MTP-off baseline.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
